theory.intro:
Cut a parallelogram along its height and slide the piece across, and it becomes a rectangle with the same base and height. So its area is base times perpendicular height.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4,0);
\coordinate (C) at (5.4,2.4);
\coordinate (D) at (1.4,2.4);
\coordinate (H) at (1.4,0);
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway,below] {$b$};
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (D);
\draw (D) -- (A);
\draw[dashed] (D) -- (H) node[midway,right] {$h$};
\coordinate (ra) at ($(H)!0.3cm!(D)$);
\coordinate (rb) at ($(H)!0.3cm!(B)$);
\draw (ra) -- ($(ra)+(rb)-(H)$) -- (rb);
\draw[->] (6,1.2) -- (6.9,1.2);
\draw (7.5,0) -- (11.5,0) node[midway,below] {$b$};
\draw (11.5,0) -- (11.5,2.4) node[midway,right] {$h$};
\draw (11.5,2.4) -- (7.5,2.4);
\draw (7.5,2.4) -- (7.5,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

